     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com (Services overview; Foreground service types; background start
     restrictions) and the kotlinx.coroutines Flow context-preservation rule on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A running app records a workout in a background coroutine. On the test phone it works. On a user's phone with twenty other apps open, the recording stops after a few minutes in the pocket, with no crash and no error. The system had no reason to keep a backgrounded process alive, because nothing told it the work mattered.</p>

A `Service` is not a thread. It is a **declaration to the system**: this process is doing work that matters, even without a visible screen. Android has narrowed what that declaration can claim, release after release, until long-running user-visible work became a typed foreground service with a notification. This chapter covers the three kinds of service, the main-thread trap, foreground service types, and when WorkManager is the better tool.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### Three kinds of service, three contracts

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-state="error"><strong>Background (started)</strong><small>restricted since Android 8: stopped soon after the app leaves the foreground</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Foreground</strong><small>visible notification; type requirements depend on OS/target SDK · higher process priority, not immunity from termination</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Bound</strong><small>lives while clients are bound · a client-server API, often over Binder</small></div></div><figcaption>Whatever its kind, a service's callbacks run on the main thread; the work itself needs its own thread or coroutine scope.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: animation -->
### Start a location-tracking foreground service, step by step

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">What each step proves to the system</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="manifest" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Manifest</strong><small data-text="manifestV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="perm" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Runtime permission</strong><small data-text="permV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="start" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Start while visible</strong><small data-text="startV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="fg" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>startForeground(…, TYPE_LOCATION)</code></strong><small data-text="fgV">—</small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="work" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>The work</strong><small data-text="workV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Declare","caption":"For this Android 14+ device and app targeting API 34+, the manifest declares foregroundServiceType=\"location\", FOREGROUND_SERVICE and FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION. Missing the required type/permission prevents this foreground promotion.","add":["manifest"],"state":{"manifest":"done"},"text":{"manifestV":"type location + permissions declared"}},
{"label":"Ask the user","caption":"On Android 14+ for this target-34+ app, location-type promotion also checks its granted location permission and applicable runtime prerequisites. While-in-use access normally requires starting while the activity is visible.","add":["perm"],"state":{"perm":"done"},"text":{"permV":"location granted"}},
{"label":"Start from the foreground","caption":"The user taps Start Workout while the app is visible. On Android 12+ for apps targeting API 31+, background foreground-service starts are restricted unless a documented exemption applies.","add":["start"],"state":{"start":"done"},"text":{"startV":"started from a visible activity"}},
{"label":"Promote with a notification","caption":"The service calls startForeground with its notification and type. The user can now see that tracking is running.","add":["fg"],"state":{"fg":"active"},"text":{"fgV":"notification shown"}},
{"label":"Do the work off the main thread","caption":"onStartCommand returns quickly; a coroutine scope owned by the service records locations. When the workout ends, the service stops itself and the notification disappears.","add":["work"],"state":{"fg":"done","work":"done"},"text":{"workV":"coroutine in the service's scope; stopSelf() at the end"}}
]</script><figcaption>This scenario assumes Android 14+ and target SDK 34+. A legal foreground-service start still does not make the process immune to termination.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Tradeoffs :: check -->
**Service or WorkManager?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Navigation with turn-by-turn voice while the screen is off.</summary>

A foreground service of the matching type: user-initiated, continuous, and visible as a notification.
</details>

<details><summary>Upload yesterday's photos when the device is on Wi-Fi and charging.</summary>

WorkManager: persist the request and reschedule eligible work after process death or reboot. Constraints and system scheduling still determine when it runs.
</details>

<details><summary>Sync a draft the moment the user taps Send, finishing even if they leave the app.</summary>

Request expedited WorkManager scheduling for short user-important work, with an explicit OutOfQuotaPolicy. It is subject to quota and system load; exhaustion may fall back to ordinary work or drop the request. It is not an immediate-start guarantee.
</details>
